---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../lib/paths';
---

<Base
  title="How to Manage Multiple AI Coding Agents - Forager"
  description="Learn how to run multiple harness-backed AI coding agents with tmux persistence, git worktrees, a TUI dashboard, and Forager's local review model."
>
  <Nav />

  <article class="pt-32 pb-16 px-6">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto prose-content">
      <header class="mb-12">
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-white mb-6 leading-tight">How to Manage Multiple AI Coding Agents</h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
          When coding agents run across several repos and branches, a session list can help you find their terminals and working directories. This guide covers Forager's dashboard, groups and Git worktrees.
        </p>
      </header>

      <section>
        <h2>The Problem with Multiple Agents</h2>
        <p>
          AI coding agents like Claude Code, Codex, Gemini CLI, and OpenCode each need their own terminal session. When you are working on a real project, you might have one agent refactoring a module, another writing tests, and a third exploring an API integration. Each runs in its own terminal, and you quickly lose track of which is which.
        </p>
        <p>
          The typical workaround is a collection of tmux sessions or terminal tabs with cryptic names. You switch between them trying to remember which agent is doing what, whether it is waiting for input, or if it finished ten minutes ago. Context switching becomes the bottleneck instead of the AI.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>A Session Manager Built for AI Agents</h2>
        <p>
          <a href={sitePath('/')}>Forager</a> lists its managed sessions in a TUI dashboard. It estimates agent activity from process and terminal observations so you can choose which session to inspect.
        </p>
        <p>
          Sessions can be organized into groups. Closing the TUI leaves their tmux processes running while the host and tmux server stay up.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Key Features for Multi-Agent Workflows</h2>

        <h3>Status Detection</h3>
        <p>
          Forager uses agent-specific process and terminal patterns to estimate whether Claude Code, Gemini CLI, OpenCode, Mistral Vibe and Codex sessions are working, waiting or idle. Labels can be stale or misclassified, especially when a CLI changes its output. Inspect the terminal and task artifacts to confirm progress or completion.
        </p>

        <h3>Git Worktrees for Parallel Work</h3>
        <p>
          Instead of cloning the same repo multiple times, Forager uses <a href={sitePath('/guides/git-worktrees-ai-development/')}>git worktrees</a> to let multiple agents work on different branches of the same repository simultaneously. Each agent gets its own working directory with its own branch, but they all share the same git history.
        </p>

        <h3>Session Groups</h3>
        <p>
          Organize your sessions into hierarchical folders. Keep client work in one group, open source contributions in another, and experimental agents in a third. Groups can be collapsed and expanded in the TUI.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Manual tmux vs. Forager</h2>
        <p>
          You can absolutely manage AI agents with raw tmux. Many developers do. But the workflow gets tedious quickly:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Without Forager</strong>: Create tmux sessions manually, name them yourself, switch between them with keyboard shortcuts, check each one individually for status, and manage worktrees by hand.</li>
          <li><strong>With Forager</strong>: <code>forager add</code> a session, see all statuses in a dashboard, navigate with arrow keys, and get automatic worktree lifecycle management.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Try the dashboard and worktree commands with your own session count and workflow to assess whether they reduce manual session management.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Getting Started</h2>
        <p>
          Install Forager with a single command:
        </p>
        <pre><code>curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kimyoungjin06/forager-cli/main/scripts/install.sh | bash</code></pre>
        <p>
          Then start the TUI with <code>forager</code> and add your first session. See the <a href={sitePath('/docs/')}>documentation</a> for the full setup guide, or read about specific features:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><a href={sitePath('/guides/git-worktrees-ai-development/')}>Git worktrees for parallel development</a></li>
          <li><a href={sitePath('/docs/hosted-harness-agents.html')}>Hosted harness agents</a></li>
          <li><a href={sitePath('/guides/tmux-ai-coding-workflow/')}>tmux workflow for AI coding</a></li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>

  <Footer />
</Base>
